---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';

const items = [
  {
    q: 'How is Cloverleaf different from Scrum?',
    a: 'Scrum assumes humans are the slow part of building software. Cloverleaf assumes AI is the primary author and humans are the slow part of <em>judgment</em>. The result is a different bottleneck and a different optimisation: Cloverleaf shifts human attention upstream into strategy and review, where the leverage now lives.'
  },
  {
    q: 'How is Cloverleaf different from Shape Up?',
    a: 'Shape Up keeps humans implementing during the cycle and uses appetites as a soft cap. Cloverleaf removes humans from implementation entirely (agents do that) and uses gates as hard caps on strategy and merge.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a single developer use Cloverleaf?',
    a: 'Yes. The 9 agents are recommended roles, not staffing. A solo dev can switch personas in a single Claude session and still get the structural benefits of fresh-eyes review and typed failure handling.'
  },
  {
    q: 'What about teams that aren\'t using AI yet?',
    a: 'Cloverleaf is specifically for teams where AI is the primary code author. If your team writes most code by hand, Scrum or Shape Up will fit better.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the methodology stable?',
    a: 'Standard v0.8.0 (this version). The architecture (two tracks, 4 work items, 9 agents) is settled. Some details may evolve based on real-team feedback.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a reference implementation?',
    a: 'Yes — <code>@cloverleaf/reference-impl</code> on npm. Both tracks ship as Claude Code skills. Delivery runs through the orchestrator <code>/cloverleaf-run</code>: the Implementer works the Task, then a single delivery council convenes. The Reviewer always sits; the Security Reviewer joins when the Task\'s security classification is high, and a high-risk Task additionally seats QA and — when the diff touches UI — the UI Reviewer. The Documenter is a step in the Task lifecycle rather than a council member. Discovery runs through <code>/cloverleaf-discover</code>, which drives RFC → Spike → Plan → Tasks across six skills.'
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I cite Cloverleaf?',
    a: 'Cite the live guide at https://cloverleaf-org.github.io/cloverleaf/ and pin to the Standard version shown in the footer (currently v0.8.0). A DOI may follow.'
  },
  {
    q: 'When should I use a Plan vs go RFC → Task directly?',
    a: '<p>Plans are checkpoints. The <code>task_batch_gate</code> exists so a human reviews the decomposition before any task materialises — it\'s the load-bearing review point of the Discovery track. Use a Plan when the work spans three or more related tasks AND there\'s real value in reviewing the decomposition as a unit. The cost of forming a Plan pays for itself in the form of catching bad scope decisions early.</p><p>RFC-direct tasks (<code>/cloverleaf-new-task --rfc=&lt;RFC-ID&gt;</code>) skip the Plan and the gate. That\'s the right call for hotfixes (one task; nothing to review at the decomposition level) and for incremental RFC progress where the operator hasn\'t yet committed to a batch shape. The walker treats RFC-direct tasks as first-class — they participate in the RFC\'s auto-advance check alongside sibling Plans (see chapter 4 § "RFC-direct tasks"). Skip the Plan only when the checkpoint wouldn\'t earn its keep.</p>'
  },
];
---
<Layout title="FAQ — Cloverleaf">
  <article class="page">
    <Eyebrow>Frequently asked</Eyebrow>
    <h1 style="color: #2D5F4E">Questions about adopting Cloverleaf</h1>

    <dl class="faq">
      {items.map(item => (
        <div class="faq-item">
          <dt>{item.q}</dt>
          <dd set:html={item.a} />
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 60px 32px 80px;
  }
  .page h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 38px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin: 0 0 48px;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .faq { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 32px; }
  .faq-item {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    padding-bottom: 32px;
  }
  .faq-item:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  dt {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 22px;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin-bottom: 12px;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-text-body);
  }
</style>
